      "id": "lpic2-capacity-inodes",
      "version": 1,
      "track": "LPIC-2",
      "topic": "Filesystem capacity",
      "unit": "linux-capacity-handover",
      "title": "Find why an empty file cannot be created",
      "summary": "Diagnose inode exhaustion, then free names without damaging retained data.",
      "objective": "Create /result with 4096 logical bytes. Leave at least 2 free inodes and 1 ordinary-available block. The five /spool-* files are expendable. Keep /audit unchanged.",
      "difficulty": "Intermediate",
      "minutes": 14,
      "steps": [
        "Inspect the supplied virtual filesystem, repair capacity and required files, then check the complete handover."
      ],
      "guide": [],
      "explanation": "One valid approach: preview and remove /spool-1, /spool-2, /spool-3; set /result to 4096 logical bytes. Check both the required file lengths and remaining ordinary-available blocks/free inodes. Other safe selections meeting these outcomes are valid.",
      "hints": [
        "Compare ordinary-available blocks and free inodes before choosing cleanup.",
        "Preview removals and retain the protected files. Count rounded blocks for each required file, plus one inode per new file.",
        "Shrinking releases blocks; removing a file also releases an inode. Check remaining headroom after all required writes."
      ],
      "sources": [
        "https://www.lpi.org/our-certifications/exam-201-202-objectives/",
        "https://www.kernel.org/doc/html/latest/filesystems/ext4/super.html",
        "https://www.gnu.org/software/coreutils/manual/html_node/df-invocation.html"
      ],
      "simplifications": "Supplied logical capacity facts only. Flat virtual names; no file contents, real device, filesystem repair, sparse allocation, quota, links, real df or host I/O. Fixed overhead and reserve are explicit inputs, not an ext4 overhead calculation. Only a narrow capacity slice of LPIC-2 filesystem maintenance.",
